1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,400
The Science of Allergies.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:11,275
Allergic diseases represent a spectrum of immune-mediated hypersensitivity reactions affecting approximately 30 percent of the global population.

3
00:00:11,275 --> 00:00:22,412
Type I immediate hypersensitivity, the most common form, involves the production of immunoglobulin E (IgE) antibodies against otherwise harmless environmental antigens.

4
00:00:22,412 --> 00:00:33,662
Upon first exposure, allergens are processed by antigen-presenting cells and presented to naive T cells, which differentiate into type 2 helper T cells (Th2).

5
00:00:33,662 --> 00:00:42,425
These Th2 cells release interleukin-4 and interleukin-13, stimulating B cells to undergo class switching to IgE production.

6
00:00:42,425 --> 00:00:50,125
IgE antibodies bind to high-affinity Fc-epsilon receptors on mast cells and basophils -- a process called sensitization.

7
00:00:50,125 --> 00:01:03,587
On subsequent exposure, allergen cross-links adjacent IgE molecules on sensitized mast cells, triggering degranulation: the rapid release of histamine, leukotrienes, prostaglandins, and tryptase.

8
00:01:03,587 --> 00:01:19,300
These mediators cause vasodilation, smooth muscle constriction, mucus secretion, and increased vascular permeability, producing the clinical symptoms of allergic rhinitis, asthma, urticaria, and, in severe cases, anaphylaxis.

9
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:24,700
The prevalence of allergic diseases has risen dramatically over the past five decades.

10
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:36,437
The hygiene hypothesis, first proposed by Strachan in 1989, suggests that reduced microbial exposure in early life biases immune development toward Th2 responses.

11
00:01:36,437 --> 00:01:43,400
Epidemiological support includes lower allergy rates among children raised on farms and in larger families.

12
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:52,737
Recent research has expanded this framework to include the roles of gut microbiome composition, skin barrier dysfunction, and environmental pollutants.

13
00:01:52,737 --> 00:02:00,575
The atopic march describes the sequential progression from eczema to food allergy, allergic asthma, and allergic rhinitis.

14
00:02:00,575 --> 00:02:08,487
Therapeutic advances include allergen immunotherapy, which induces immune tolerance through regulatory T cell activation.

15
00:02:08,487 --> 00:02:20,087
Biologic therapies including omalizumab (anti-IgE) and dupilumab (anti-IL-4 receptor alpha) represent significant progress for moderate-to-severe allergic disease.

